An effect's @mount whose raise a window's @error catches has that handler
inlined into it, and the handler writes the window's state. The mount is a
package func by the time the Go targets emit it, and PackageStateFuncs --
which decides whether one becomes a Model method -- walked its body with
ir.Walk, which does not follow Call.ResolvedHandler. So the mount came out as
`func __effect0_mount()` reading `m.caught` on bubbletea, fyne and gtk4, and
none of them compiled. The same happens under a boundary.

A golden that carries a test function does not show it: the test harness makes
the component under test the Model's root, whose funcs are Model methods
anyway, so this fixture has none.

deny * `(?m)^func __effect0_mount\(` -- the mount writes the Model, so it is a method on it

-- mount.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

func risky(n int) int {
    if n > 1 {
        error.raise("too big", "range")
    }
    return n * 10
}

var caught = ""

window(@error(e) { caught = e.message }) {
    effect(@mount { risky(3) })
    text(value=caught)
}

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
        fyne
        gtk4
        html
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}
